course: dictionary
lesson: 2397
title: Cursive and joined-up writing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Cursive means joined-up handwriting. In cursive, the letters of a word are linked by flowing strokes, so the pen moves on without lifting off the paper. «We are learning cursive» means we are learning to write with our letters joined together.
explain: Cursive can also describe things, so it works as an adjective. «Cursive writing» is writing with joined, flowing letters, and «a cursive font» is a computer font that looks like joined-up handwriting. In each phrase, cursive tells you how the writing looks.
explain: Context helps you decide which meaning fits. If a teacher says «write in cursive», they mean write with joined letters, not printed capitals. Look at the words around cursive: if it is a thing you learn, it is a noun; if it describes writing, it is an adjective.
fact: Cursive means joined-up handwriting, where the letters of a word are linked by flowing strokes.
fact: Cursive can be a noun, as in «We are learning cursive», or an adjective, as in «cursive writing».
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, children usually call it joined-up writing and learn it at about ages 6 to 8.
fact: Handwriting lessons practise entry and exit strokes, the small lines that join one letter to the next.
fact: «Handwriting still matters: in tests a marker can only mark what they can read, and joined-up writing (cursive) helps speed and even spelling.»
exercise: In «We are learning cursive», what does cursive mean? | Printed capital letters | A type of paper | A kind of pencil | *Joined-up handwriting
exercise: In «cursive writing», what does cursive describe? | Writing done only in capital letters | *Writing with joined, flowing letters | Very quiet writing | Writing done with a pencil
exercise: What do children in the UK usually call cursive? | *Joined-up writing | Speed typing | Block capitals | Printing
test: Which sentence uses cursive as a noun? | «Her cursive letters flow.» | «Cursive writing is neat.» | «A cursive font looks lovely.» | *«We are learning cursive.»
test: According to «Handwriting still matters», why does handwriting matter in tests? | Printed letters are always marked wrong | *A marker can only mark what they can read | Typing is never allowed in tests | Cursive is a kind of paper
exam: Which meaning fits cursive in «a cursive font»? | Written in pencil | Made of paper | Printed in capital letters only | *Looking like joined, flowing handwriting
